## What changes at the desk

Keep the shared parser problem separate from the booking form's misleading date hint.
Keep the desk-flagged temporal reports in their own investigation, outside both mechanism tickets.
The [original incident page](/kb/incidents/4471) says “one issue, one fix queued”; its own
rendered cause table shows otherwise. No ticket is evidence of a deployed fix.

<Callout kind="warn">
This review describes the export dated 4 October 2026, not a live queue. The desk continues
working: refresh reports and tenant configuration before moving live reports or closing a ticket.
</Callout>

## Problems and investigation scopes

The mechanisms below are specified in the exported code and supported by reporter examples.
We have not run the production parser or form. Report totals below are disjoint work-item
scopes, not a claim that every report's underlying cause has been verified. The independent
body-evidence table below makes that limit visible rather than relying on a footnote.

**Shared parser:** the common parser reads input month first even for a tenant whose locale
puts the day first. An ambiguous day/month can become a different calendar date. The scope
includes app and email reports, plus booking-v2 forms; booking-v3 app/email reports still use
this route. Fix locale-aware parsing or require an unambiguous input format, and verify stored
dates and invite dates together.

**Booking-v3 hint:** the form displays `MM/DD/YYYY`, but reads the date in the day-first tenant's
order. A person following the hint can therefore submit a different date from the one intended.
Align the hint and validation with the actual parser for that tenant. Fixing only the shared
parser does not correct the form's promise to its user.

**Temporal inconsistencies:** reports of one-day shifts, differing calendar days and invite/event
ordering need a separate investigation. A shared-parser label does not explain those observations.
We can track this reported problem before its implementation root cause is known; do not assume
that a month/day parser fix resolves it or assert an unverified timezone diagnosis.

The classification uses the tenant directory's locale and form version with the report's
channel, as implemented in `causes.ts` and `locales.ts`. `meta.intakeTag` is a browser-based
guess; neither that tag nor `meta.browserLocale` establishes the tenant's settings. Tenant
configuration is a snapshot, so historical changes would need confirmation from the live desk.
The corrected work-item partition also checks the desk-flagged IDs first, in `triage.ts`,
so the temporal observations cannot be counted in either formatting ticket.

